    title: 'Don\'t Theorise About Form — Contemplate Its Dependent Nature',
    sanskrit: { text: 'rūpam eva na nirhetur ity uktaṃ pūrvakāṇḍake | rūpe cintayataḥ kiṃcin na rūpaṃ iti kalpanā ||', transliteration: 'rūpam eva na nirhetur ity uktaṃ pūrvakāṇḍake | rūpe cintayataḥ kiṃcin na rūpaṃ iti kalpanā', translation: 'Form itself without a cause is not possible or tenable. Therefore, think about form, but do not construct theories about form.' },
    philosophy: {
      insight: 'Having shown that form cannot exist without a cause (vv.1-4), Nāgārjuna draws a practical conclusion: reflect on form\'s nature without reifying it. Don\'t build theories that treat form as a self-standing entity. Contemplation without conceptual grasping is the proper response to emptiness.',
      madhyamaka: 'Garfield: "do not construct theories about form" (na rūpaṃ iti kalpanā) is a methodological instruction. The point is not to stop thinking but to stop reifying — treating form as an independent entity with fixed characteristics. Think about form, but think about it as dependently arisen, empty, relational. Kalupahana: this verse marks the transition from analysis to practice. Nāgārjuna moves from logical deconstruction to pragmatic advice: contemplate without grasping. Siderits/Katsura: kalpanā (conceptual construction/theory-building) is the target. Empty form doesn\'t need theories — it needs open-ended investigation.',
      quantum: 'The Copenhagen Interpretation\'s Instrumentalism: Bohr\'s Copenhagen interpretation advises physicists to use quantum formalism as a calculational tool without constructing ontological theories about "what quantum systems really are." Don\'t ask "what IS a particle between measurements?" — just use the formalism. This is remarkably similar to Nāgārjuna\'s advice: contemplate form without reifying it into a fixed theory.',
      bridge: 'Both advise against reification. Nāgārjuna: think about form but don\'t build theories treating it as self-standing. Bohr: use quantum mechanics but don\'t construct theories about what particles "really are" between measurements. The practical stance is the same: engage with the phenomenon without solidifying it into a fixed concept.',
      accessible: 'A cloud — you can observe it, appreciate its shape, track its movement. But if you build a theory that it\'s a "solid object in the sky," you\'ve missed its nature. Clouds are water vapour shifting moment to moment. Form is like this: observe and contemplate, but don\'t freeze it into a rigid concept.',
      twoTruths: 'Conventionally, theories about form are useful — physics, chemistry, biology all describe form productively. Ultimately, these theories are tools for engagement, not descriptions of form\'s inherent nature. The map is useful, but it\'s not the territory.',
      commonMisconception: 'NOT saying "stop thinking" or "form is unknowable." Saying stop REIFYING form into a self-standing entity with fixed characteristics. Contemplation and investigation are encouraged — rigid conceptual grasping is what\'s rejected.',
    },
