    deeperDive: [
      { q: 'Why can\'t cause and effect be identical?', a: 'If cause and effect were identical, nothing would change. The cause IS the effect. A seed IS the tree. Clay IS the pot. But clearly something changes — the seed becomes a tree, which is different from the seed. If they were truly identical, causation would be meaningless — nothing new would ever arise. The Sāṃkhya school (satkāryavāda) held that effects pre-exist in their causes; Nāgārjuna rejects this.', realLifeExample: 'If dough and bread were identical, baking would do nothing. But baking clearly changes dough into bread — something new appears. Identity makes causation impossible.' },
      { q: 'Why can\'t they be totally different?', a: 'If cause and effect were totally different — sharing nothing — then any cause could produce any effect. Why would a seed produce a tree rather than a rock? The causal connection would be arbitrary. Total difference severs the link that makes causation intelligible. There must be SOME continuity between cause and effect.', realLifeExample: 'If a recipe and its dish were totally unrelated, any recipe could make any dish. But a cake recipe makes cake, not soup. The similarity (ingredients → dish) is what makes the causal connection work.' },
      { q: 'How do quantum state transitions illustrate this?', a: 'When an electron absorbs a photon and jumps to a higher energy level, the "before" and "after" states share some properties (same electron, same atom, same charge) while differing in others (different energy level, different orbital). The transition is neither identity (it changed) nor total difference (it\'s the same electron). This partial identity/partial difference is the quantum analogue of Nāgārjuna\'s middle way.', realLifeExample: 'You before and after a haircut: same person (not identical change) but different appearance (not total sameness). Some things stay, some change.' },
      { q: 'What schools does this verse target?', a: 'Sāṃkhya (satkāryavāda): effects pre-exist in their causes, so cause and effect are similar (same substance). Vaibhāṣika (asatkāryavāda): causes perish and effects arise fresh, so they\'re dissimilar (different moments). Nāgārjuna rejects both: neither similarity nor dissimilarity captures the relationship. Both views reify the cause-effect relationship.', deeper: 'Garfield: this verse is directed at specific Indian philosophical positions but has universal applicability. Any account that reduces causation to either identity or difference will fail.' },
      { q: 'What\'s the "middle way" of causation?', a: 'The middle way acknowledges that cause and effect are related without being identical, and different without being disconnected. The relationship is real but can\'t be reduced to a simple category. This is why causation is empty — not because it doesn\'t happen, but because it transcends the concepts we use to describe it.', realLifeExample: 'A river flowing — the water at point A is related to the water at point B (same river, same flow) but not identical (different water molecules, different moment). The river IS the flow, not a fixed thing.' },
      { q: 'Does this undermine scientific laws of causation?', a: 'No. Scientific causal laws describe regularities — what follows what under what conditions. They work perfectly well at the conventional level. What Nāgārjuna challenges is the METAPHYSICAL interpretation — that there are inherently existing causes producing inherently existing effects through inherently existing causal powers. The regularities are real; the metaphysical picture is empty.', deeper: 'Kalupahana: Nāgārjuna\'s analysis is consistent with Humean causation — regularities without inherent causal power. The middle way of causation is description without reification.' },
    ],
    quiz: {
      beginner: {
        question: 'Can cause and effect be identical or totally different?',
        options: ['A) Yes, they are identical', 'B) Neither — if identical, no change occurs; if totally different, no connection exists. Both extremes fail', 'C) Yes, they are totally different', 'D) The question doesn\'t matter'],
        correct: 'B',
        explanation: 'Both identity and total difference are rejected. Cause and effect have a middle-way relationship: connected but distinct, related but not reducible to sameness or otherness.',
      },
      intermediate: {
        question: 'How do quantum state transitions illustrate the "neither same nor different" principle?',
        options: ['A) Quantum states never change', 'B) Before and after states share some properties (same electron) while differing in others (different energy level) — neither identical nor totally different', 'C) All quantum transitions are identical', 'D) Quantum mechanics doesn\'t involve state changes'],
        correct: 'B',
        explanation: 'An electron jumping energy levels preserves some properties (same electron, charge) while changing others (energy, orbital). The transition is partial identity and partial difference — the quantum middle way.',
      },
      advanced: {
        question: 'Which Indian schools does this verse specifically target?',
        options: ['A) Only Buddhist schools', 'B) Sāṃkhya (effects pre-exist in causes = similar) and Vaibhāṣika (causes perish, effects arise fresh = dissimilar) — both are rejected', 'C) Only materialist schools', 'D) No specific schools'],
        correct: 'B',
        explanation: 'Sāṃkhya held cause-effect similarity (satkāryavāda). Vaibhāṣika held dissimilarity (asatkāryavāda). Nāgārjuna rejects both: the cause-effect relationship transcends identity and difference.',
      },
    },
